  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The initial NbMoTaW refractory HEA has a nearly single-phase, largely BCC matrix without significant secondary phases or nanoprecipitates.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adding approximately 0.5–1 at.% carbon is a suitable interstitial-alloying intervention for an NbMoTaW-based refractory HEA.",
      "field": "intervention",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC5227964::web::001::body"
      ],
      "same_system": "unclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Homogenization after carbon addition will distribute carbon uniformly.",
      "field": "intervention",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Carbon addition and homogenization will produce controlled nanoscale carbide precipitates.",
      "field": "intervention",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cold rolling and annealing will produce a fine microstructure in the proposed alloy.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Carbon interstitial alloying will result in uniformly dispersed nanosized carbide precipitates.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Carbon interstitial alloying will refine the grain boundaries.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Fine carbide precipitates hinder dislocation motion and thereby increase strength.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Carbide precipitates locally modify stacking-fault energies.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The carbon-induced microstructure facilitates nanotwinning or transformation-induced plasticity.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC5227964::web::001::body"
      ],
      "same_system": "unclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Additional TWIP or TRIP deformation will improve plasticity while precipitate strengthening is retained.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC5227964::web::001::body"
      ],
      "same_system": "unclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Relative to carbon-free NbMoTaW, the carbon-alloyed material will have higher yield strength.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC5227964::web::001::body"
      ],
      "same_system": "unclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Relative to carbon-free NbMoTaW, the carbon-alloyed material will have greater uniform elongation.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC5227964::web::001::body"
      ],
      "same_system": "unclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Interstitial carbon can reduce the usual strength–ductility trade-off in an HEA.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC5227964::web::001::body"
      ],
      "same_system": "unclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "The simultaneous strength and ductility improvement is expected under room-temperature, quasi-static deformation after the stated thermomechanical processing.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Excessive carbon or processing that creates coarse carbides may eliminate the benefit and cause embrittlement.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The optimal carbon concentration and long-term or elevated-temperature stability of the nanoprecipitates remain uncertain.",
      "field": "uncertainty",
